    "spokenContext": {
      "en-L02-S05": "Now assume a body mass of sixty kilograms. Divide the daily intake, sixteen thousandths of a milligram, by sixty. The result is approximately zero point zero zero zero two six seven milligrams per kilogram of body mass per day. If a hypothetical acceptable daily intake is one hundredth in the same units, this food contributes about two point seven percent. That percentage covers only the calculated pathway. It establishes neither acceptable total exposure nor residue-limit compliance.",
      "en-L02-S06": "The acceptable daily intake is a reference for repeated intake over a lifetime, not permission for a large dose on one day. Acute exposure assessment uses a different time scale and an appropriate acute reference value when established for the substance. People also differ in body mass and food consumption. Real assessments consider consumption distributions, sensitive groups, residue definitions and uncertainty. The simple example teaches the calculation sequence; it does not replace a complete assessment."
    }
